WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:03.210
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:06.420
I help some cloudy file
servers clear their performance

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.020
thoughts.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.270
Will they see clearly
once the fog is gone?

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.600
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.415
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:16.800
Nimbusdisk is a Cloud based
file system company who

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.050
is having some trouble
with their persistent disk

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:19.980
performance.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.500
They tried all the
usual suggestions

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.500
like analyzing disk
size and CPU count,

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.930
but still weren't seeing
the performance they wanted.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.090
And while digging
around for other things

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.770
they might be able
to try, I actually

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.690
stumbled into this little gem.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.070
It says, the default block
size on volumes is 4K.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:42.480
For throughput oriented
workloads, values of 256k

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.410
are recommended.

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.590
Now, unbeknownst to me,
this single sentence

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.130
was exactly what was
causing the Nimbusdisk woes.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.040
So generally speaking, when
it comes to disk operations

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.190
a block is the
smallest unit of data

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.170
that is read during
an I/O operation.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.370
So if you're trying
to read 5 bytes

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.280
and your block
size is 512 bytes,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.660
the entire 512 bytes
will be read from disk,

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.600
even though your application
will only use 5 bytes from it.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.870
So with this in mind,
it's important to observe

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:13.260
the following formula with
respect to disk performance.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.920
Now, how this evens out is that
the IOPS and the block size

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.710
tend to have an inverse
relationship to each other.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.140
As block size
increases, it takes

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.090
longer to read a
single block and thus

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.240
the number of IOPS decreases.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.060
Inversely, small block
size yields higher IOPS.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.390
To figure out how GCP's
persistent disks behave

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.050
as the block size changed I
set up a simple 32 core machine

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.110
with a 1 terabyte standard
persistent disk attached to it

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.540
and tested the performance
against block sizes.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.690
What we see, expectedly,
is that as the window

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:50.670
size grows our IOPS get lower
and our throughput gets higher.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.440
Likewise, we can
do the same test

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.830
for an SSD persistent disk.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.510
And we see a similar
reduction in IOPS

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.130
as block size increases.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.400
But, by the way, that hard
cap you see for throughput

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.830
at 16k window size is
actually a function

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.260
of the testing
config I was using.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:12.040
By increasing our PD SSD
side, or moving to 64 cores,

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.340
we should be able to
remove that limit.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.750
Now here's the important part.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.480
By default, GCP's
persistent disks

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.590
use a block size of 4k which
is perfect for higher IOPS

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.840
workloads like
relational databases.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.910
Nimbusdisk, however,
was much more concerned

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.440
with throughput,
which meant they

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:31.980
wanted to be on the other
side of the block size window.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.850
With a simple config change to
move to a 1 megabyte blocksize,

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.180
their perf increased about 30x.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.280
Not bad for a simple
command line flag.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.210
If you'd like to know
more about this problem,

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:45.610
check out the article listed
in the description below.

